in the eukaryotic proteasome. ... Lon is responsible for the degradation of the cell division inhibitor SulA; lon mutants are UV sensitive, due to the stabilization of SulA. lon mutants are also mucoid, due to the stabilization of another Lon substrate, the positive regulator of capsule transcription, RcsA. ... The genes encoding ClpQ and ClpY, hslVU, initially were identified as heat shock genes by Chuang et al. (5) and subsequently were selected in searches for multicopy plasmids that perturb heat shock regulation by sigma E and suppress the effects of a mutation in htrC in causing sensitivity to amino acid analogs (38).\n\nResearch Article\n\n15 June 1999\n\n# Redundant In Vivo Proteolytic Activities ofEscherichia coli Lon and the ClpYQ (HslUV) Protease\n\nAuthors: Whi-Fin Wu, YanNing Zhou, Susan GottesmanAuthors Info & Affiliations\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1128/jb.181.12.3681-3687.1999\n\nCite\n\nPDF/EPUB\n\n## ABSTRACT\n\nThe ClpYQ (HslUV) ATP-dependent protease of Escherichia coli consists of an ATPase subunit closely related to the Clp ATPases and a protease component related to those found in the eukaryotic proteasome. We found that this protease has a substrate specificity overlapping that of the Lon protease, another ATP-dependent protease in which a single subunit contains both the proteolytic active site and the ATPase. Lon is responsible for the degradation of the cell division inhibitor SulA; lon mutants are UV sensitive, due to the stabilization of SulA. lon mutants are also mucoid, due to the stabilization of another Lon substrate, the positive regulator of capsule transcription, RcsA. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRole of HrcA and CIRCE in the heat shock regulatory network of Bradyrhizobium japonicum - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10613857/)\nciteturn20search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 26.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; doi: 10.1128/JB.182.1.14-22.2000. ... A large number of bacteria regulate chaperone gene expression by the CIRCE-HrcA system in which a DNA element called CIRCE serves as binding site for the repressor protein HrcA under non-heat-shock conditions.We have cloned the two consecutive genes hrcA and grpE of Bradyrhizobium japonicum by using a complementation approach that screened for GrpE function.In vivo and in vitro transcript mapping demonstrated that both genes are transcribed separately from RpoH (sigma(32))-dependent promoters.\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Institut für Mikrobiologie, Eidgenössische Technische Hochschule, CH-8092 Zürich, Switzerland.\n\n  * PMID: 10613857\n  * PMCID: PMC94234\n  * DOI: 10.1128/JB.182.1.14-22.2000\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n[Button: Full text links ]Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat [Select]\n\n## Abstract\n\nA large number of bacteria regulate chaperone gene expression by the CIRCE-HrcA system in which a DNA element called CIRCE serves as binding site for the repressor protein HrcA under non-heat-shock conditions. We have cloned the two consecutive genes hrcA and grpE of Bradyrhizobium japonicum by using a complementation approach that screened for GrpE function. In vivo and in vitro transcript mapping demonstrated that both genes are transcribed separately from RpoH (sigma(32))-dependent promoters. To investigate the supposed negative regulatory function of HrcA, we compared the expression of putative target genes in the wild type with that in an hrcA mutant. Transcription of the CIRCE-associated chaperonin operons groESL(4) and groESL(5), as well as the beta-galactosidase activity derived from corresponding groE-lacZ fusions, was strongly elevated in the hrcA mutant even at physiological temperatures. Expression of other heat shock regulons (RpoH or ROSE dependent) was not affected. To study the activity of HrcA in vitro, we purified a histidine-tagged version of the protein under nondenaturing conditions. Specific binding to the CIRCE element was obtained with a soluble fraction of HrcA in gel retardation experiments.\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n10.1128/JB.182.1.14-22.2000. (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=ce2358fe827905b64bd7fd77219ec25b8dd5f05a&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn20search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; cum RNA polymerase, and to Michael Kowarik for puriﬁed B. japoni- ... dation for Scientiﬁc Research and the Federal Institute of Technology, ... The heat-shock-regulated grpE gene of ... are involved in the heat shock regulation of chaperonin gene expression in ... tion of the transcription machinery for housekeeping genes of Bradyrhizo- ... 10. ... erone complexes, DnaKJ-GrpE and GroESL.\nVOL. 182, 2000\nHrcA-CIRCE INTERACTION\n21\nserved even in the absence of GroES and ATP (25). The\nprevention of aggregate formation in the presence of urea\nmight enable partially unfolded HrcA protein to reach an\nactive DNA-binding conformation and, hence, a measurable\ngel retardation activity with a CIRCE fragment. Experiments\nwith C. acetobutylicum HrcA (OrfA), which had been puriﬁed\nunder denaturing conditions, revealed that HrcA aggregation\nwas prevented by the addition of DnaK, DnaJ, or the whole\nDnaK chaperone machinery (39), indicating that urea-dena-\ntured HrcA is a substrate not only for the GroE chaperone\nmachinery but also for the DnaK system. While the speciﬁc\ninteraction between the CIRCE element and HrcA is well\nestablished now, it becomes evident that resolution of the\nmore-debated matters of a physical interaction between HrcA\nand GroEL and the thermosensing by HrcA must await further\nstudies.\nACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nWe are indebted to Debbie Ang for providing plasmid pBW401 and\nthe E. coli strains DA258 and DA259, to Christoph Beck for B. japoni-\ncum RNA polymerase, and to Michael Kowarik for puriﬁed B. japoni-\ncum RpoH2-H6 protein. We thank Christopher Kaestner for help in\nautomatic DNA sequencing and Michael Spring, Stephanie